The period from 1538 to 1544 covers the founding of [[Tamsui]] and the establishment of the [[Sinchew Colony]]. It begins with the [[Treaty of Anping]] and the creation of the settlement in 1538, continues through the [[First Mahik War]] and the destruction of the northern chiefdoms, and ends with the royal edict of 1 June 1544 which divided the Zong colonial territory and constituted the northwestern coast as a colony in its own right.

The period is the shortest treated in the history of the territory and the most consequential. In six years a trading outpost at a bay became the capital of a colony of the Zong crown, and the peoples of the coast were destroyed or reduced to clients of the settlement built among them.

== Treaty of Anping and the founding of Tamsui (1538) ==

{{Main|Treaty of Anping}}

The [[Treaty of Anping]] was concluded in 1538 between the Zong colonial administration at [[Anping]], represented by the Governor-General Ông Bûn-hêng, and the [[Hachinawa|Hahkina state]] of the Lurandian interior, represented by its king Tsulhachi. Under it the Zong recognised the Hahkina as a sovereign polity and renounced territorial claims to the interior, in exchange for a fixed coastal presence and a formal system of trade and arms supply.

The treaty also provided for a joint campaign against the chiefdoms of the region north of the Hahkina frontier, whose lands were to be divided between the two parties.

The settlement of [[Tamsui]] was founded in the same year. It was the harbour for which the site had been acquired from the [[Sūn-heng Hâng]] in 1535, and it stood on land the Akwesa had granted for ten pigs in 1511.

The legal ground on which the Zong disposed of the coast beyond the settlement was supplied in the course of the treaty negotiations. [[Hokta]], the miko of the [[Akwesa]], who had died in 1527, was declared posthumously to have been King of the Northern Mahiks — the ruler of the whole region rather than of the Akwesa alone. On that basis the administration held that the northern chiefdoms had been subject to Hokta, that Hokta had granted the land to the Sūn-heng Hâng, and that the company had conveyed it to the colony in 1535. The chiefdoms were consequently described as subjects in rebellion rather than as foreign powers. The title is not attested in any document of Hokta's lifetime.

== First Mahik War (1538–1542) ==

{{Main|First Mahik War}}

The war began on 11 August 1538, four months after the conclusion of the treaty, and was fought on two fronts: Zong forces operating against the coastal chiefdoms to secure the Tamsui harbour, and Hahkina forces advancing north from their own frontier.

The Zong committed approximately 4,000 colonial troops and sustained 430 killed and 1,430 wounded. The Hahkina committed some 45,000 men and sustained 11,340 killed and 18,432 wounded. The disparity reflects the scale of the two contributions and the nature of the campaign, in which Zong operations were confined chiefly to the coastal positions.

The war ended on 7 April 1542 with the defeat of the chiefdoms and the partition of their territory between the two allies. The north, including Tamsui and the coast, passed to the Zong; the south, adjoining the Hahkina frontier, passed to the Hahkina. Between eighty and ninety per cent of the population of the region was killed or enslaved, and survivors fled north and west into the territories of the [[Timara]] and the [[Salowi]].

One chiefdom came through the war intact. The [[Akwesa]], which had granted the Tamsui site and whose bay had passed to the colony by purchase, had become a client of the colony before the war and survived as a minority community within the territory. The modern collective name for the peoples of the region, [[Kawesa]], descends from the name of that single chiefdom.

== Supply of the settlement (1542–1543) ==

The four years of the war were the settlement's best provisioned. Tamsui was the base for the Zong campaign on the northern coast, and troops, provisions and material passed through it in quantity.

The war ended in April 1542 and the flow of supplies ended with it. The settlement was supplied thereafter on the terms on which the whole colony was administered: all goods and people bound for Tamsui were required to pass through [[Anping]] first, so that the northwestern settlement received what remained after the older colony had taken its share. Its applications for stores and for the means to maintain the works it had built were answered slowly, and its petitions for permission to import directly from [[Zong]] were refused.

A body of opinion at Anping held that the northern territory was a waste of the colony's resources, that the war had been fought for a harbour that did not repay its cost, and that the settlement's difficulties were of its own making. Those who held that view were in a position to act upon it. The correspondence of the period records repeated applications from Tamsui and repeated delays at Anping.

It is against this that the position of the settlement's governor must be understood. [[Chiu Khéng-gio̍k]] had ruled the territory as the polity of [[Okheng]] from 1527 until its conveyance in 1535, trading directly with Zong and answerable to no authority but his own. As provisional administrator of the colony he was answerable to Anping, and by 1542 he was worse supplied than he had been as the ruler of a private kingdom.

== Tamsui Plot (1543–1544) ==

{{Main|Tamsui Plot}}

From about 1542 the administration at Tamsui ceased to observe the restriction on direct trade. Private merchants trading out of Zong were engaged to carry cargo to the settlement, bypassing the requirement that it pass through Anping. The traffic ran for about a year and was conducted with the participation of the settlement's merchant houses.

It was discovered at Anping by 11 August 1543. On that date the administration at Anping held evidence that Tamsui had been trading directly with the mainland.

On 4 September 1543 [[Chiu Khéng-gio̍k]] despatched an envoy from Tamsui to the mainland in secret, instructed to petition the court at [[Huwei]] for the governance of the territory on terms of its own. The passage to Huwei is some 10,711 km, a voyage of four to five months; the envoy's ship therefore reached Zong early in 1544, by which time its sender was already under arrest. The petition did not seek separation from the colony; it sought relief from the requirement that the settlement's trade pass through Anping.

Anping had already acted. A force was despatched from the older colony, and on 20 November 1543 it placed Chiu Khéng-gio̍k under arrest at Tamsui. The administration reported the matter to Zong, characterising what had occurred as an attempt by the governor to restore his own polity. That characterisation was not one the papers of the settlement support.

He was held at Tamsui for the whole of the period that followed, and it was the arrival of the royal edict that ended his detention.

The petition and the report reached the court at about the same time. Receiving two irreconcilable accounts of the same events, the court did not act upon either, and despatched its own investigators to the colony to determine the facts. The inquiry established that no force had been raised at Tamsui and no territory seized, that the officers of the settlement had neither deposed nor resisted the governor, and that the substance of the dispute was the terms on which the settlement was supplied.

== Royal edict (1544) ==

The matter came before the [[Societal Assembly]] of Zong, and was resolved by separating the two settlements. The division was made by royal edict, deliberated in the assembly and approved by [[Tiancheng of Zong|Emperor Tiancheng]].

The edict was issued at [[Huwei]] on 1 June 1544. The passage from Tamsui to Huwei is some 10,711 km, a voyage of four to five months, and the edict did not take effect in the colony until [[11 November]] of that year. The colony was accordingly divided in law from June and in fact from November, and the same promulgation that constituted the Sinchew Colony released the man it had been sent to depose.

The territory northwest of the [[Qingjiang]] river, centred on Tamsui, was constituted as the [[Sinchew Colony]]. The territory southeast of the river, centred on Anping, remained the [[Louyang Colony]]. Each was thereafter governed by its own governor and reported directly to the court.

The settlement was administrative and territorial rather than disciplinary. When the edict took effect at Tamsui on [[11 November]] 1544, [[Chiu Khéng-gio̍k]] was released. He had then been in custody for about twelve months. He was not tried, and no penalty is recorded against him. The office of provisional administrator was dissolved, and a replacement administrator was sent from Anping to take charge of the northwestern territory under the new arrangement. He was [[Tân Bûn-khéng]], who had been the administration's negotiator at the Treaty of Anping in 1538 and had devised the posthumous title on which the war had been fought. He became the first governor of the Sinchew Colony and held the office until his death in 1546, of a disease the record does not identify.

Chiu Khéng-gio̍k was appointed the colony's ambassador to the Akwesa, and held that office for the remainder of his life.

== Aftermath ==

The division did not end the administrative tensions it had resolved. The two colonies retained distinct interests and distinct relationships with the court, and the political divergence between them widened over the following century.

The [[Akwesa]] remained a minority community within the colony, and Chiu Khéng-gio̍k was the colony's intermediary with them from 1544 until his death in 1598. The lands he had ruled as a polity became a colony of the Zong crown, and the people who had been subject to him became a minority in a territory governed by a power he represented and did not command.

[[Chiu Bûn-hái]], the founder of the Sūn-heng Hâng and the man who had taken the grant of 1511, died at Tamsui on 9 June 1543, eight months before the discovery that began the crisis and before the division that ended it. He had seen his son sell the territory in 1535 and govern it for the colony for eight years, and did not live to see him lose it.

